id: question-packets/mascot
title: "Mascot ranked questions"
kind: question-packet
status: proposed
updated: 2026-09-07

purpose: >
  Hold the stack-ranked questions the YAWN.bot mascot and skin system must
  answer, in the same shape yawn.bot/frontier renders: one governing
  question, ranked entries, each with the current answer, the case against
  it, the proof that would settle it, and the loop status. Public entries
  render on the unprovisioned /mascot view; every entry renders for a
  provisioned owner.

target_contract: interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn
render_format: "ranked details/summary list (yawn.bot/frontier pattern); only the top rank open by default"
ranking_rule: "ranked by how much the answer changes Dave's next move; the ranking is a bet, not evidence"
ranking_note: "q-space/protocol-v1.yawn defines a frontier score; this packet ranks by hand until the app computes one"

governing_question: "What is the YAWN.bot mascot, and how does it know what it is doing?"

honesty_line: >
  These answers are proposals with their status attached. An answer marked
  inferred or reported has not been proved in this runtime yet.

rules:
  - "A question with no answer stays visible with answer: null."
  - "An answer never upgrades its own epistemic status; proof does."
  - "public_safe: false entries never render on the unprovisioned view."
  - "loop_status uses the six-stage loop: signal | orientation | choice | move | proof | update."
  - "Links whose href starts with src/, public/, nestheads-art-engine/, or YAWNS/ point into the yawn-ai/yawn.bot repository, not this one."

questions:
  - rank: 1
    key: universal_browser_technology
    question: "What is the best universal browser technology for a state-driven pixel mascot in the YAWN.bot Next.js app?"
    answer: >
      Four renderers sharing one manifest and one state map, none of them
      new dependencies. The header mark is a CSS sprite strip (steps(N) on
      transform, image-rendering pixelated, a data-mascot-state attribute,
      an accessible name) that renders correctly in server HTML with no
      JavaScript on the hot path. State cards are the same strip with
      layered body, hat, and accessory strips on one duration. The pixel
      field behind them is a small Canvas 2D cell buffer (about 96 by 12
      cells for a band) updated at 8 to 12 fps by one shared ticker, with a
      CSS gradient and steps() mask fallback for no-JavaScript and reduced
      motion. Swarms use exactly one canvas per view: Phaser 4 sprites on
      the existing WebGL context inside the world, a Canvas 2D batch
      renderer outside it. Sheets are authored size-native at 32, 128, and
      256 pixels.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: >
      CSS animations cannot share a ticker, cannot be tinted per instance,
      and keep running off-screen unless the subtree uses
      content-visibility; the field and the icon can drift out of phase.
      The layer split is a compromise that must be measured, not assumed.
    proof: "A header mark, a state card, and a 200-body world view run on iOS Safari, Android Chrome, and desktop with one shared ticker, crisp pixels, reduced-motion stills, and zero route-owned console errors."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Sprite manifest the runtime already detects", href: "src/domain/sprite-sheets.ts" }
      - { label: "Existing CSS steps() idle fallback", href: "src/app/globals.css" }
      - { label: "WebKit WebGL context limit (16 per page)", href: "https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/blob/main/Source/WebCore/html/canvas/WebGLRenderingContextBase.cpp" }
      - { label: "Phaser 4 renderer notes", href: "https://phaser.io/news/2026/04/phaser-4-renderer-faster-cleaner-and-built-for-modern-games" }

  - rank: 2
    key: how_frames_are_made
    question: "How are the animation frames made?"
    answer: >
      By translation, not invention. A skin's animation set (Will P) is the
      pose library. Each skin frame is translated into the YAWN robot with
      the pose, frame box, and pivot preserved, using the robot sheets as
      identity references. Two lanes: a hosted frontier editor for the
      first cycles (OpenAI image edits with the skin frame as the edited
      image plus two to four existing pairs and the robot sheet as
      references, transparent output, token-priced at roughly USD 0.13 to
      0.25 per frame at high quality once reference inputs are billed, no
      seed returned), and a local
      deterministic lane as the system of record (an open FLUX.2 Klein base
      with a lineart or canny control adapter taking the skin frame as
      structure and the robot frame as identity, plus a paired edit adapter
      trained on the existing pairs, run through the app's loopback ComfyUI
      adapter with seeds recorded). A seeded multi-reference edit and
      pixel-native tools are fallbacks; video is not used for eight-frame
      loops. Every lane ends in the same deterministic
      post-processing (background removal, palette lock, nearest-neighbor
      downscale, pivot alignment, gutters, one horizontal strip), the same
      alpha-column detection, and three scores (pose fidelity, identity,
      grid fidelity) that go on the receipt.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: >
      Hosted editors give no seed, so a cycle cannot be regenerated
      byte-identically, and no general model reliably emits a full
      grid-snapped sheet in one call; per-frame translation multiplies
      calls and drift. The local lane fixes both but needs a trained
      adapter and a GPU that does not exist in production.
    proof: "One skin cycle travels reference pack, pose pack, brief hash, approval receipt, candidate frames, post-process, manifest, review, promotion receipt with every hash reconciling and pose fidelity above the review threshold."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Image generator receipt idiom", href: "src/lib/yawn-image/generate.ts" }
      - { label: "Art engine", href: "nestheads-art-engine/README.md" }
      - { label: "FLUX.2 Klein license and references", href: "https://github.com/black-forest-labs/flux2" }
      - { label: "Sprite sheets with gpt-image-2 (developer forum)", href: "https://community.openai.com/t/developing-sprite-sheets-with-gpt-image-2/1379831" }

  - rank: 3
    key: training
    question: "How do we train a model on our character?"
    answer: >
      Start from the twenty pairs that already exist (eight front walk,
      eight back walk, four idle, skin frame beside robot frame at 256 by
      256). Train a paired edit adapter on those pairs (skin frame as
      control, robot frame as target) over an open reference-control
      adapter, with captions naming the state and direction, using a
      checked-in ai-toolkit config, hosted (about USD 9 to 17 per run) or
      about one hour on a 24 GB local GPU.
      Every accepted translation adds a pair, which is how the art engine's
      20 to 40 image target fills. Reference conditioning alone is enough
      for one mascot's identity; the adapter is what makes the pipeline
      self-hosted, seed-reproducible, license-clean, and able to generalize
      to cycles the robot has never been drawn in.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: >
      Twenty pairs of one character in three cycles may overfit to walking
      and idling and fail on a sitting or coding pose; the adapter could
      copy the old sheets rather than learn the mapping. Hold out one cycle
      and test before trusting it.
    proof: "A held-out skin cycle produces identity-stable, pose-faithful robot frames under a fixed rubric, with cost and time recorded, and the run is reproducible from its seed and dataset hashes."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: false
    links:
      - { label: "LoRA registry", href: "nestheads-art-engine/model/lora-registry.json" }
      - { label: "ai-toolkit trainer", href: "https://github.com/ostris/ai-toolkit" }
      - { label: "Paired frames", href: "public/nestheads/characters/" }

  - rank: 4
    key: generator_migration
    question: "When must the image generator move off its current model, and to what?"
    answer: >
      The app calls gpt-image-1 today. The provider has scheduled that model
      for removal on 2026-10-23, with gpt-image-2 as the documented
      replacement, and the smaller and mid models follow on 2026-12-01. The
      translate-frames adapter therefore targets gpt-image-2 from its first
      line, and the existing one-press generator migrates before the
      removal date. gpt-image-2 is token-priced, accepts up to sixteen
      input images, and returns real alpha in preview.
    epistemic_status: reported
    case_against: "Provider dates move, and this one was read from trackers rather than the provider's own page, which the session could not reach. Confirm on the provider's deprecations page before planning the cutover week."
    proof: "A dry run against gpt-image-2 returns the same receipt shape with real usage, and the old model string is gone from the generator before the removal date."
    loop_status: move
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Generator to migrate", href: "src/lib/yawn-image/generate.ts" }
      - { label: "Provider deprecations", href: "https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/deprecations" }

  - rank: 5
    key: claude_technology
    question: "What technology does Claude use for the pixel mascot and the animated Ultracode band?"
    answer: >
      In the Claude Code CLI, read from the shipped binary: a React terminal
      renderer derived from Ink with Yoga layout that paints ANSI truecolor
      text cells. The mascot is a nine by three grid of Unicode block
      glyphs in one orange, with four poses and entrance sequences stepped
      at 60 ms per frame, off under reduced motion. The ultracode row
      colors each character's background from a radial cosine wave over an
      eight-step violet ramp, wavelength twenty cells, ticking every 80 ms.
      No shader, dither, sprite, or image asset is involved. The iOS band
      in the recording is undocumented; its grammar matches the CLI ripple
      (a violet wave on a coarse cell grid plus the same figure as pixels).
      The web mascot animations have been recreated by others with SVG
      frame sequencing; the originals are not inspectable without a
      session.
    epistemic_status: observed
    epistemic_note: "observed for the CLI; unknown for iOS; reported for web and desktop"
    case_against: "Even the confirmed answer describes Anthropic's stack, not ours; the useful part is the two-layer split (field plus figure), which is already observable and cheap to reproduce on the web."
    proof: "A logged-in device or browser capture names the iOS and web mechanisms; until then the answer stays partial."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Ultracode is a setting, not a model effort level", href: "https://code.claude.com/docs/en/model-config" }
      - { label: "Mascot glyph grid reported by users", href: "https://github.com/anthropics/claude-code/issues/24926" }
      - { label: "Web mascot recreated with SVG and GSAP", href: "https://tympanus.net/codrops/2026/05/05/reverse-engineering-claude-ais-mascot-animations-with-svg-and-gsap/" }

  - rank: 6
    key: how_state_is_known
    question: "How does the mascot know what state it is in?"
    answer: >
      It does not decide; it reads. One pure function resolves the loop
      status, worker expression state, mirror-face expression, world
      execution state, actor state, statement tone, holarchy lifecycle,
      coordinate frame status, header status, and inbox status that the
      runtime already keeps into {state, variant, flags} plus a replayable
      input hash. Hard-gate flags outrank needs-owner, which outranks the
      loop status, which outranks micro-expressions. Missing or stale
      inputs resolve to sleeping with an unknown badge; nothing is guessed.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Eleven source enums drift independently; a mapping table can silently rot, and today's only mapping (running or reviewing means walk, else idle) lives in two Phaser runtimes. The mitigation is one resolver and an exhaustive test that fails on any unmapped value."
    proof: "The state-map test enumerates every value of every input enum; adding a value without a mapping fails CI."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Loop statuses", href: "src/domain/yawn-state-substrate.ts" }
      - { label: "Worker expression state", href: "src/domain/automation-registry.ts" }
      - { label: "Mirror face expression", href: "src/domain/yawn-frame-grid.ts" }
      - { label: "World execution states", href: "src/domain/world-coordinate-types.ts" }

  - rank: 7
    key: which_states
    question: "Which states exist, and what does each one look like?"
    answer: >
      Ten states: sleeping, orienting, asking, moving, working (a variant
      of moving for code, write, and read moves), proving, updating,
      holding, blocked, closed. Four flags overlay them: needs_owner,
      error, local_only, unknown_inputs. Two states already have sheets
      (sleeping from the idle sheet or the browser-face body, moving from
      the walk sheets); the rest fall back to the nearest accepted ancestor
      until a translated sheet is promoted.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Ten states may be too many to read at 32 pixels; a swarm may only need four legible silhouettes. Keep the ten in the model, but prove legibility per size before translating ten cycles."
    proof: "A legibility test: five people name the state of each still frame at 32, 128, and 256 pixels; states below a threshold merge visually (not semantically)."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "State model", href: "interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn" }

  - rank: 8
    key: dormant_and_working_bodies
    question: "What is the dormant sprite, and what is the working one?"
    answer: >
      Dormant is the little browser character: the robot with a
      browser-window head and the yawn.ai mark on its chest, shown as a
      static image on /new and in the header today, with a CSS bob when
      walking. Working is the walking robot sheet (eight frames at 20 fps)
      that was translated frame for frame from the skin's walk cycle. Both
      are YAWN robots, but they are not the same design: the dormant body
      has a rectangular monitor head, a boxy torso, and cylindrical limbs,
      while the walking body keeps Will's round head with a CRT window and
      a cyan body. The idle sheet (four frames at 8 fps) is the breathing
      version of the walking body, not of the dormant one. The image
      generator's house references anchor on the dormant body today, so
      translation of walking cycles must anchor on the walking sheets.
    epistemic_status: observed
    case_against: "Two dormant bodies (the browser-face still and the idle breath sheet) are different designs and will read as two characters in one header. Dave must declare the walking robot the canonical skinned body and keep the browser-face as a separate sleeping asset, or commission one dormant frame in the walking identity as the first approved translation."
    proof: "The manifest names one accepted sheet per size for sleeping and for moving, and the header, /new, and the world all read them."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Dormant body on /new", href: "src/components/bot/yawn-world-bot.tsx" }
      - { label: "Walking sheet", href: "public/nestheads/characters/yawn-front-walk.png" }

  - rank: 9
    key: skins_over_a_bot
    question: "How does a skin go over a yawn.bot?"
    answer: >
      A skin is a complete base animation set drawn once as a character. It
      has two jobs: it is the pose library the robot's own state sheets are
      translated from, and, once translated, it can be dressed over a bot
      as an avatar option. Dressing a skin is an accepted projection
      preference at principal, arena, or yawn scope that swaps the body
      layer only. The state grammar, the flags, the labels, and the field
      never change with the skin. Every skin record names its rights holder
      before anything is translated.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Skins invite identity drift: a swarm of different bodies is harder to read than a swarm of one body with hats. Cap public skins to rights-cleared sets and keep the root bot's body canonical on shared surfaces."
    proof: "A dressed skin renders every state with the same labels and flags as the root body, and a signed-out view never shows a private skin."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Skin system", href: "interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn" }
      - { label: "Preference contract", href: "schemas/projection-preference.v1.schema.json" }

  - rank: 10
    key: who_approves
    question: "Who approves translation, spend, training, and promotion?"
    answer: >
      The owner of the coordinate, per bounded batch, with a receipt that
      names the brief hash, lane and adapter, candidate count, spend
      ceiling, and expiry. Translation, training, and promotion are
      separate receipts. Fidelity scores and visual quality never approve
      anything; a nonconforming candidate ends its grant.
    epistemic_status: observed
    case_against: "Per-candidate approval is slow for forty cycles times retries. The answer is a bounded batch receipt (one cycle or one state at one size), never standing authority."
    proof: "A translation, training, or generation attempt without a current receipt is refused and leaves an inspectable refusal record."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Owner-decision receipt precedent", href: "records/interception-statement-image-approval-2026-08-29.yawn" }
      - { label: "Art engine boundary", href: "nestheads-art-engine/AGENTS.md" }

  - rank: 11
    key: header_icon
    question: "What does the upper-left icon show, and to whom?"
    answer: >
      The current bot's mascot in its current state, with the state name as
      accessible text, at exactly 32 pixels inside the 40 pixel brand
      control the header already has (the state name goes into the
      control's accessible name; the image stays hidden from assistive
      technology). Until a server-written root-bot state record exists,
      the public mark shows sleeping and says so. On the
      public root it shows only the root bot's public state class. A
      signed-in owner on their coordinate sees that coordinate's bot, with
      flags.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "A moving icon in every header is attention cost; the chrome contract says default surfaces foreground one state and one next action. The mark must stay quiet: small, slow idle, no motion under reduced motion, and no motion at all for sleeping unless hovered or focused."
    proof: "Signed-out Playwright run finds no private detail in the header DOM; reduced-motion run finds no running ticker."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Chrome contract", href: "interface/yawn-chrome-v1.yawn" }
      - { label: "Header brand slot", href: "src/components/chrome/yawn-global-chrome.tsx" }

  - rank: 12
    key: holarchy_inheritance
    question: "How do child yawns and bots inherit the mascot?"
    answer: >
      By default every descendant uses the same state map and manifest
      schema, so a swarm reads consistently. Accepted projection
      preferences at principal, arena, or yawn scope may change which
      sheet, skin, or field pattern a state uses; exact-coordinate
      revisions win over ancestors. Private state, reasons, and receipts
      never inherit through nesting. The app has no inheritance resolver
      yet; one is added in the domain layer rather than read ad hoc.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Inheritance makes a hundred identical bodies; identity may need to be per-yawn (hat, accessory, skin) to stay legible. Roles and flags supply that variety without forking the state grammar."
    proof: "Holarchy inheritance tests: a child renders the parent's accepted default, an exact-coordinate override wins, and a private parent flag never reaches a public child."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Holarchy inheritance policy", href: "YAWNS/Policies/YAWN_HOLARCHY_INHERITANCE.yawn" }

  - rank: 13
    key: hats_and_accessories
    question: "What do hats and accessories mean?"
    answer: >
      Hats encode role (root steward, objective steward, worker). Badges
      encode flags (needs owner, error, local, unknown). Tools encode the
      active move kind (terminal, pen, book, mail). Bubbles carry one short
      text label. An accessory adds a visual role and never remaps a state.
      No hat or accessory concept exists in the app yet.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Accessory vocabularies grow without limit and stop meaning anything. Cap the vocabulary per kind and require a meaning string for every accessory in the manifest."
    proof: "Every accessory in a published manifest has a meaning string, and the state label still renders with the accessory hidden."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 14
    key: public_vs_provisioned
    question: "What does a public visitor see, and what does Dave see?"
    answer: >
      Public: the mascot in its public state, the state list with one still
      each, and the public-safe questions, in the /frontier format with no
      JavaScript required. Provisioned: everything public plus the full
      ranked set with cases against and proofs, live state resolution
      inputs, the manifest inspector, pending translation proposals with
      dry-run cost, registered skins, and accepted defaults with their
      scope. The route follows the existing /dave pattern: the session
      decides the public mission or the owner surface on one path.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Two views of one route can leak by accident when a component forgets the boundary. Render the provisioned extras from a server component behind the session check, never by hiding client-side."
    proof: "Signed-out and signed-in Playwright receipts diff the DOM; the signed-out DOM contains none of the provisioned markers."
    loop_status: choice
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Frontier page pattern", href: "src/app/frontier/page.tsx" }
      - { label: "Public plus owner on one route", href: "src/app/dave/page.tsx" }

  - rank: 15
    key: accessibility_and_cost
    question: "How does this stay accessible and cheap on phones?"
    answer: >
      One shared ticker per page; sprites share decoded textures; off-screen
      and hidden-tab bodies stop; every raster layer uses nearest-neighbor
      scaling; prefers-reduced-motion shows a still frame and a still
      field; the state name is always text; the header mark never holds a
      WebGL context; no layer flashes faster than three times a second and
      reduced motion removes jitter and ripples entirely; a user pause
      control is proposed for loops longer than five seconds.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Reduced motion is an OS query many users never set, and a pause control takes a chrome slot the contract keeps minimal. Measure attention cost before deciding which one carries the duty."
    proof: "A Playwright performance receipt on a throttled mobile profile: main-thread time per frame under budget with 50 bodies visible, and a reduced-motion run with no running ticker."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Pause, Stop, Hide", href: "https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/pause-stop-hide.html" }

  - rank: 16
    key: truthfulness
    question: "What proves the mascot is telling the truth about state?"
    answer: >
      The resolved state, its inputs, its freshness, and the input hash
      render together in the provisioned view, and the same inputs must
      resolve identically on every device. The mascot is a View: it cannot
      be more true than the runtime records it reads, and it cannot be less
      true without failing the replay check.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "A stale input (a worker whose status did not update) makes an honest mascot show a wrong state. Freshness is an input: stale inputs add the unknown badge."
    proof: "Replay test: recorded inputs replayed later resolve to the same state and hash; inputs older than the freshness window show the unknown badge."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Projection boundary", href: "core/projection-and-aperture.yawn" }

  - rank: 17
    key: mascot_changes_pixels
    question: "Can the mascot change the pixels of the interface, or turn them into code?"
    answer: >
      As expression, yes: in the working state the field cells drain into
      the terminal as code, and in updating the body dissolves and
      re-resolves. As authority, no: the mascot animates a View and never
      mutates semantic state, permissions, or records. The pixels it
      changes are its own layers.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "The metaphor invites a mascot that appears to act, which is the hidden-authority problem in costume. The rule holds only if every visible change maps to a recorded event the user can inspect."
    proof: "Every animation transition corresponds to a state change with a source event; a transition with no source event is a test failure."
    loop_status: orientation
    public_safe: true
    links: []

  - rank: 18
    key: not_allowed
    question: "What is not allowed?"
    answer: >
      No writing state from the mascot. No color-only or motion-only state.
      No translation, training, spend, storage, or publication without a
      current receipt. No third-party mascot copies and no skin without a
      rights holder. No private detail on public surfaces. No preference or
      skin that remaps a canonical state. No WebGL context in the header
      and no canvas per bot.
    epistemic_status: observed
    case_against: null
    proof: "Each rule has a test or a refusal record in the contract's proof section."
    loop_status: proof
    public_safe: true
    links:
      - { label: "Contract boundaries", href: "interface/yawn-mascot-v0.1.yawn" }

  - rank: 19
    key: first_move
    question: "What is the smallest first move?"
    answer: >
      Ship the state map and the header mark with the existing sheets
      (sleeping from the idle sheet or the browser-face body, moving from
      the walk sheet) and the CSS field band behind a route flag. No
      translation, no spend. Then translate one skin cycle (idle or sit
      into working) through the full receipt chain to prove the pipeline
      before the other cycles. One approved probe cycle (front walk, eight
      frames, ceiling USD 2) compared against the measured 0.995 overlap of
      the existing pairs decides whether the hosted lane is trusted.
    epistemic_status: inferred
    case_against: "Shipping the mark before the states exist trains users to ignore it. Keep the mark quiet until at least three states are legible."
    proof: "State-map test green; header mark visible with text label; reduced-motion still; public root unchanged; one promoted sheet with a reconciled receipt chain."
    loop_status: move
    public_safe: true
    links: []

replay:
  next_questions:
    - "Which two states, after sleeping and moving, most change what a viewer does next?"
    - "Does a per-yawn body or a per-parent accessory read better in dense views?"
    - "Which cycles does the full Will P library contain, and which state does each map to?"
